  "claim": "An English writer argued that women ought to have an education in her book from 1792. This writer was friends with Fanny Blood.",
  "evidencia": "Page: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. Sentence: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman: with Strictures on Political and Moral Subjects (1792), written by the 18th-century British proto-feminist Mary Wollstonecraft, is one of the earliest works of feminist philosophy.\nPage: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. Sentence: She argues that women ought to have an education commensurate with their position in society, claiming that women are essential to the nation because they educate its children and because they could be \"companions\" to their husbands, rather than mere wives.\nPage: Mary Wollstonecraft. Sentence: Mary Wollstonecraft ( ; 27 April 1759 – 10 September 1797) was an English writer, philosopher, and advocate of women's rights.\nPage: Fanny Blood. Sentence: Frances \"Fanny\" Blood (1758 - November 29, 1785) was an English illustrator and educator and longtime friend of Mary Wollstonecraft.",
